First, we need to choose which one of the six dice will roll a 1. There are $\binom{6}{1} = 6$ ways to do this.
Then, we need to choose which one of the six dice will roll a 2. There are $\binom{6}{1} = 6$ ways to do this.
Now, for the remaining 4 dice, we want them to not be 1 or 2. The probability of rolling anything that is not 1 or 2 is $\frac{4}{6} = \frac{2}{3}$.  Since we have 4 dice, the number of ways to roll the remaining 4 dice so that they are not 1 or 2 is $\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^4 = \frac{16}{81}$.
So the number of ways to roll exactly one 1 and exactly one 2 is $\binom{6}{1} \times \binom{6}{1} \times \left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^4 = 6 \times 6 \times \frac{16}{81} = 36 \times \frac{16}{81} = \frac{576}{81} = \frac{64}{9}$.
The probability is $\frac{\text{number of favorable outcomes}}{\text{total number of outcomes}} = \frac{\frac{64}{9}}{6^6} = \frac{64}{9 \times 46656} = \frac{64}{419904} = \frac{8}{52488} = \frac{2}{13122} = \frac{1}{6561}$.

