$\sin^{-1}(\sin 4) = 4$ because $4$ is in the range of $\sin^{-1}x$ which is $\left[-\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2}\right]$. In particular, $4 \approx 2.56 \notin \left[-\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2}\right]$, so $\sin^{-1}(\sin 4) = \pi - 4$.
$\sin^{-1}(\sin 5) = 5$ because $5$ is in the range of $\sin^{-1}x$ which is $\left[-\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2}\right]$. In particular, $5 \approx 2.89 \notin \left[-\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2}\right]$, so $\sin^{-1}(\sin 5) = \pi - 5$. 
We have $\sin^{-1}(\sin 3) + \sin^{-1}(\sin 4) + \sin^{-1}(\sin 5) = 3 + (\pi - 4) + (\pi - 5) = 3 + \pi - 4 + \pi - 5 = 2 \pi - 6$.

The step-by-step solution is as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $\sin^{-1}(\sin 3) = 3$, since $3 \in \left[ -\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2} \right]$.
    \item $\sin^{-1}(\sin 4) = \pi - 4$ because $4 \notin \left[ -\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2} \right]$.
    \item $\sin^{-1}(\sin 5) = \pi - 5$ because $5 \notin \left[ -\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2} \right]$.
\end{enumerate}
\begin{align*} \sin^{-1} (\sin 3) + \sin^{-1} (\sin 4) + \sin^{-1} (\sin 5) &= 3 + (\pi - 4) + (\pi - 5) \\ &= 3 + \pi - 4 + \pi - 5 \\ &= 2 \pi - 6\end{align*}
In this case, the units are radians.

