\begin{document}

How may functions area both even and odd? (Assume that the domain of the function as the set of all real numbers.)

Let $f(x)$ be a function such that $f(-x) = f(x)$ and $f(-x) = -f(x)$.
We want to find the number of functions that are both even and odd.

If a function $f(x)$ is even, then $f(-x) = f(x)$ for all $x$.
If a function $f(x)$ is odd, then $f(-x) = -f(x)$ for all $x$.

For a function to be both even and odd, we must have $f(-x) = f(x)$ and $f(-x) = -f(x)$.
Thus, $f(x) = -f(x)$, which implies $2f(x) = 0$, so $f(x) = 0$ for all $x$.
This is the only function that is both even and odd.

Therefore, the number of functions that are both even and odd is 1.

